 \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380935 1113 #uuid "6663394a-d271-5f09-bd80-f481874766bb" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380935 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $0.00$ | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380937 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380938 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380939 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380940 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1041 "field-1" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380940 13194139536048 true] [17592186380936 1110 #uuid "c0808640-b442-5a9a-a263-40fabec6ef61" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380937 1034 "t{e}^{3t}" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536048 true] [17592186380937 1111 #uuid "3df03b28-58fd-5bda-88e5-bf2dce7eba1f" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380938 1034 "{e}^{3t}" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536048 true] [17592186380938 1111 #uuid "ad164a18-b7fd-51bb-9cf6-859f915c1257" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380939 1034 "t{e}^{-3t}" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536048 true] [17592186380939 1111 #uuid "d4552c42-cd23-5dcb-905e-4b0101152bf0" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380940 1034 "{e}^{-3t}" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536048 true] [17592186380940 1111 #uuid "7c055298-a974-57d1-9d69-0c3be9ed2717" 13194139536048 true] [17592186380941 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380942 13194139536048 tru